# Patch 0064 — the last light/dark authority: Zen writes the toolbox text
# colour in JS, and it never heard about the Kavacha theme.
#
# SYMPTOM (user, 2026-08-03): "where are my tabs? all i did was make it vertical
# then clicked the collapse button. also, when it was uncollapsed, the text was
# unreadable because the text was overlayed or something weird. Also the theme
# changing doesnt even work."
#
# Three reports, ONE root cause. The tabs were never missing and the collapse
# button was never broken: with Kavacha Daylight active, the entire vertical
# strip was being painted in near-white on a near-white surface. Collapsed, only
# the icons remain — also white — so the strip reads as empty. Expanded, the
# labels are there but invisible, which is "the text was overlayed or something
# weird". And since the sidebar is the most theme-coloured surface in the
# window, a theme switch that leaves it looking identical reads as "theme
# changing doesnt even work".
#
# 1. WHY 0061 DID NOT ALREADY FIX THIS. 0061 made CSS agree with the
#    `kavacha-theme-mode` attribute, and it does — measured under Daylight,
#    `color-scheme` computes `light` on the root, on `panel`, on `menupopup`,
#    on `#navigator-toolbox`, on `#urlbar` and on the content browser, and
#    `--zen-branding-paper` correctly resolves to the theme surface. The urlbar
#    text goes properly dark. But `--toolbox-textcolor` is not decided by CSS at
#    all. ZenGradientGenerator.mjs computes `isDarkMode` and writes
#    `--zen-primary-color`, `--toolbox-textcolor` and `--toolbar-color-scheme`
#    INLINE on the document element. Its `isDarkMode` getter switches on
#    `zen.view.window.scheme`, whose value 0 means "always dark" and is the
#    shipped default. So the theme could never influence it.
#
#    Zen then spends that variable in four places that a normal rule cannot
#    outrank: `--toolbar-color` and `--toolbox-textcolor-inactive`
#    (zen-theme.css, both !important), and `zen-workspace`'s own `color` plus
#    `--tab-selected-textcolor` (zen-workspaces.css), which mix it 95% with the
#    accent.
#
#    MEASURED BEFORE, Daylight active, sidebar expanded: all 21 text-bearing
#    elements in the strip — the Space indicator, the Clear button, all five
#    tabs, their labels, their sublabels, the new-tab button and its icon —
#    computed to color(srgb 0.962 0.949 0.946 / 0.81), luminance 0.893, against
#    a surface luminance of 0.927. Contrast ratio ~1.04:1.
#    AFTER: 0 of 44 text-bearing chrome elements below 4.5:1, worst ratio 8.55.
#    Midnight worst 10.74, Forest worst 9.92, both also 0 failures.
#
#    THE FIX, in two halves, because one alone is not enough:
#      * kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css points --toolbox-textcolor at the theme's
#        own --kavacha-text-primary at 80% (the same alpha Zen uses for its two
#        literals). This needs !important — not for specificity, but because the
#        declaration it must beat is an INLINE one, and important author
#        declarations are the only kind that outrank a style attribute. One
#        declaration serves both modes: --kavacha-text-primary is already dark
#        under light themes and light under dark ones, and has already been
#        through the engine's WCAG guard, so it is a colour known to read.
#      * KavachaThemeEngine._stampMode now also writes `zen.view.window.scheme`
#        to match the derived mode. The CSS half makes the strip readable; this
#        half repairs the value at its SOURCE, so Zen's own
#        getAccentColorForUI(accent, isDarkMode) runs with the right mode. It
#        was previously turning the user's #F76F53 into rgb(99, 44, 33) — a
#        muddy near-brown — under a light theme.
#
#    WHY NOT A PREF ALONE: setting zen.view.window.scheme by itself leaves the
#    already-written inline values stale until Zen next runs onWorkspaceChange,
#    so the strip stays unreadable until an unrelated event repaints it. WHY NOT
#    CSS ALONE: it corrects the text but leaves --zen-primary-color derived for
#    the wrong mode. Both halves, or the bug half-survives either way.
#
#    PRIVATE WINDOWS are untouched: Zen forces --toolbox-textcolor on
#    `&[zen-private-window='true']` at (1,3,0), which outranks this rule's
#    (1,1,0). A private window looking different is a privacy signal.
#
# 2. THE COLOUR PARSER STILL HAD A SILENT NULL. parseLiteralColor read #rgb,
#    #rrggbb and rgb()/rgba() — but the style system returns anything that has
#    been through color-mix() as `color(srgb r g b / a)` with 0..1 channels.
#    That returned null, and null in _stampMode means "leave the appearance mode
#    unchanged", so a theme whose surface was built from a color-mix() would
#    silently keep the PREVIOUS theme's light/dark. This is the same defect
#    class 0061 set out to kill and missed one syntax of. Found because the
#    verification probe written for this patch had the identical gap and
#    confidently reported zero unreadable elements on a strip where every one of
#    the 21 was unreadable.
#
# 3. THE SIDEBAR WIDTH RULE FOUGHT ZEN'S SPLITTER. kavacha-layout.inc.css set
#    `width: var(--kavacha-sidebar-width, 250px) !important` on
#    #navigator-toolbox for every user in vertical mode, because the engine
#    always stamps `kavacha-sidebar`. That !important also beat the width Zen's
#    #zen-sidebar-splitter writes on drag, so dragging the sidebar edge snapped
#    straight back. It further fired against the 60px max-width clamp
#    vertical-tabs.css applies while collapsed — harmless in the used value,
#    but ours to stop asking for. Now gated on a new
#    `kavacha-sidebar-width-custom` attribute the engine stamps only when the
#    layout document's sidebarWidth differs from the shipped 250, AND on
#    [zen-sidebar-expanded="true"]. MEASURED: toolbox width was pinned at 250px
#    before; it now reports Zen's own 230px, and collapse/expand cycles
#    230 -> 60 -> 230 with all five tabs present at every step.
#
#    This restores the "inert at the defaults" contract the rest of that layer
#    already keeps.
#
# VERIFIED over Marionette (chrome context, TCP 2828) against the fast build,
# after a restart — .sys.mjs modules load once per process:
#   * 18-step layout matrix: horizontal/vertical/arc, collapse and expand in
#     each, sidebar left/right, density compact/comfortable. 5 visible tabs at
#     every step; tabStyle, theme mode and zen.view.window.scheme agree at every
#     step (0 for Midnight and Forest, 1 for Daylight).
#   * Contrast sweep per theme, each colour alpha-composited over the theme
#     surface before the ratio: 0 failures below 4.5:1 across all three
#     built-ins, worst 8.55 / 10.74 / 9.92.
#   * Appearance panel: opens at 360x649, bottom 707 in a 758px window; 3 theme
#     cards, 10 accent swatches, 12 segments; worst in-panel text 8.11:1.
#     Clicking a theme card moved kavacha.theme.active and the live tokens;
#     clicking an accent swatch moved zen.theme.accent-color and
#     --zen-primary-color together.
#   * about:studio under Daylight: kavacha-theme-mode=light, color-scheme light,
#     body background rgb(247, 246, 251), body text rgb(27, 24, 48).
#
# WHY A PATCH: all four files are themselves delivered by earlier Kavacha
# patches (0022, 0056, 0061) into upstream Zen's src/ tree; there is no overlay
# or pref layer that can reach --toolbox-textcolor, because the value being
# corrected is written by upstream JS as an inline style.
#
# FILES: src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css
#        src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-layout.inc.css
#        src/zen/common/sys/KavachaThemeEngine.sys.mjs
#        src/zen/common/sys/KavachaLayoutEngine.sys.mjs
#
diff --git a/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-layout.inc.css b/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-layout.inc.css
--- a/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-layout.inc.css
+++ b/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-layout.inc.css
@@ -35,7 +35,28 @@
    tab strip is active. Left is the baseline. */
 /* stylelint-disable-next-line media-query-no-invalid */
 @media -moz-pref("zen.tabs.vertical") {
-  :root[kavacha-sidebar]:not([kavacha-sidebar="hidden"]) #navigator-toolbox {
+  /* Sidebar width. Gated THREE ways, and each gate closes a real defect
+     (patch 0064):
+
+       [kavacha-sidebar-width-custom]  the engine stamps this only when the
+         layout document's sidebarWidth differs from the shipped 250. Without
+         it this rule fired for everyone, and `width: 250px !important` beat
+         the width Zen's #zen-sidebar-splitter sets when the user drags it --
+         so dragging the sidebar edge snapped straight back and read as a
+         broken splitter.
+
+       [zen-sidebar-expanded="true"]   collapsing sets
+         max-width/min-width: 60px !important on the same element
+         (vertical-tabs.css). The clamp meant the used width came out right
+         anyway, but we were still asking for 250px on every frame of the
+         collapse animation against a 60px cap. Not our width to set once the
+         sidebar is collapsed.
+
+       :not([kavacha-sidebar="hidden"])  unchanged; hidden has its own rule. */
+  :root[kavacha-sidebar-width-custom][zen-sidebar-expanded="true"]:not(
+      [kavacha-sidebar="hidden"]
+    )
+    #navigator-toolbox {
     width: var(--kavacha-sidebar-width, 250px) !important;
   }
 
diff --git a/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css b/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css
--- a/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css
+++ b/src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-theme-scheme.inc.css
@@ -52,6 +52,8 @@
 #main-window[windowtype='navigator:browser'][kavacha-theme-mode='light']:not(
     [privatebrowsingmode='temporary']
   ) {
+  --toolbar-color-scheme: light !important;
+
   &,
   & panel,
   & menupopup,
@@ -76,6 +78,8 @@
 #main-window[windowtype='navigator:browser']:not(
     [kavacha-theme-mode='light']
   ) {
+  --toolbar-color-scheme: dark !important;
+
   &,
   & panel,
   & menupopup,
@@ -86,3 +90,50 @@
 %include schemes/dark.inc.css
   }
 }
+
+/* --- Toolbox text colour (patch 0064) -------------------------------------
+   THE BUG 0061 DID NOT REACH. Everything above is CSS, and it works: with
+   Daylight active, `color-scheme` measures `light` on the root, on `panel`, on
+   `menupopup` and on the content browser, and --zen-branding-paper correctly
+   resolves to the theme surface. But the tab strip stayed white-on-white,
+   which is what "where are my tabs" actually was -- the tabs were painted, in
+   near-white, on a near-white surface.
+
+   The reason is that `--toolbox-textcolor` is not decided by CSS at all.
+   ZenGradientGenerator.mjs writes it INLINE on the document element from its
+   own `isDarkMode` getter, which switches on `zen.view.window.scheme` (0 =>
+   always dark, and 0 is the shipped default) and has no idea a Kavacha theme
+   exists. Zen then spends it in three places that all carry !important or feed
+   a mix: `--toolbar-color` (zen-theme.css), `--toolbox-textcolor-inactive`
+   (same), and `zen-workspace`'s own `color` plus `--tab-selected-textcolor`
+   (zen-workspaces.css), which mix it 95% with the accent.
+
+   Measured under Daylight before this rule: every one of the 21 text-bearing
+   elements in the sidebar -- the Space indicator, the Clear button, all five
+   tabs, their labels, their sublabels, and the new-tab button and its icon --
+   computed to color(srgb 0.962 0.949 0.946 / 0.81), luminance 0.893, against a
+   surface luminance of 0.927. That is a contrast ratio of about 1.04:1.
+
+   THE FIX. Point it at the theme's own primary text instead, at the same 80%
+   Zen uses for its own two literals (rgba(0,0,0,.8) / rgba(255,255,255,.8)).
+   One declaration serves both modes because --kavacha-text-primary is already
+   dark under light themes and light under dark ones, and it has already been
+   through the engine's WCAG guard, so it is a colour we know reads.
+
+   WHY !important, when nothing else in this file needs it: the declaration it
+   must beat is an inline one. The cascade puts important author declarations
+   above normal declarations from a style attribute, so this is the only form
+   that can win -- specificity alone never can.
+
+   Private windows are deliberately NOT excluded here: Zen forces
+   `--toolbox-textcolor: rgba(255,255,255,.8) !important` on
+   `&[zen-private-window='true']` at (1,3,0), which outranks this rule's
+   (1,1,0), so private windows keep their forced-dark text with no help from
+   us. Same reasoning as the private-window carve-out above. */
+#main-window[windowtype='navigator:browser'] {
+  --toolbox-textcolor: color-mix(
+    in srgb,
+    var(--kavacha-text-primary) 80%,
+    transparent
+  ) !important;
+}
